Here's a simple Java program that uses Jsoup to parse HTML and generate HTML:

```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;

public class PublicTest {
    public String generateHtml(String html) {
        return Jsoup.parse(html, "http://www.example.com", null).body().html();
    }

    public void testParse() {
        String html = "<p>This is a paragraph of text.</p>";
        Document document = generateHtml(html);
        System.out.println(document.body().text());
    }
}
```

To compile this code, you can use the following command:

```bash
javac PublicTest.java
java PublicTest
```

This will create an executable file called `PublicTest.jar` that you can run to test the code. The output of the program will be the parsed HTML.

You can also add some additional functionality to the `generateHtml()` method, such as removing tags or adding new ones:

```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;

public class PublicTest {
    public String generateHtml(String html) {
        Document document = Jsoup.parse(html);
        return document.body().html();
    }

    public void testParse() {
        String html = "<p>This is a paragraph of text.</p><span>This is a span element</span>";
        System.out.println(generateHtml(html));
    }
}
```

This will output:

```html
<p>This is a paragraph of text.</p>
<span>This is a span element</span>
```

You can also add error handling to the `generateHtml()` method to handle cases where the input HTML is malformed or cannot be parsed:

```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;

public class PublicTest {
    public String generateHtml(String html) {
        try {
            Document document = Jsoup.parse(html);
            return document.body().html();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to parse HTML: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    public void testParse() {
        String html = "<p>This is a paragraph of text.</p>";
        System.out.println(generateHtml(html));
    }
}
```
